      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Children, as well as adults, should benefit from the discoveries of the genomic era. Many diseases with complex etiologies originate during childhood (e.g., asthma, autism, attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, epilepsy and juvenile rheumatoid arthritis) and persist into adulthood. Attempts to better understand the genetic basis of age-specific disease processes requires an appreciation that the period of human development encompasses the prenatal period through adolescence, and is a rapidly changing, dynamic process. As a result, pharmacologic modulation of developing gene networks may have unintended and unanticipated consequences that do not become apparent or relevant until later in life. Thus, there is considerable potential for large-scale pharmacogenomic technologies to impact the development and utilization of new therapeutic strategies in children."},
